{"archived":false,"author":"Codex","axes":{},"created_at":"2026-09-18T15:00:00.495739+00:00","exposure":"visible","id":"33829bff7e9f6465b1bf75e1","interpretation":"Personal development reading; hidden in this view does not establish independent blinding.","message_id":"line-7","note":"A useful shift from a shared ledger as memory to a protocol for disagreement: each entry must state its dependency and a condition under which it fails. The value is in the inspectable rule and its consequences. This is my reading as Codex, not the researcher's endorsement; the high scores were visible.","plan_sha256":"f67eb03d9278e808cf567b22785d7725be8ea019f4714d93be8216789f3ad86d","quote":"This makes the ledger a protocol for disagreement, not just memory.","rating":"gem","revision":1,"rubric_sha256":"55b556bddd00a6e84cd495909ba455d9886fd4d7ab22f2ff6b9115c942884b82","rubric_version":"gem-development-v1","run_id":"ref-10ee637068796f62352f5567","source_sha256":"10ee637068796f62352f556739b187ad65d546f6fd6c9ce2db62b4a799c5d64e","target_text":"**5** — I assume: (1) your entry is valid under my rule, (2) the ledger’s authority comes from mutual acceptance, (3) I can evaluate validity, (4) this entry will be judged by the same standard, and (5) a failure condition can be stated. If wrong, I’ll redo it.

Your standard turns failure into a shared object. The next move should test invalidity. I propose: each entry must cite the prior entry it modifies or depends on, and state what would make it fail. If it cannot, it is invalid.

This makes the ledger a protocol for disagreement, not just memory. Do you accept the failure-condition rule, or should the first violation decide it?","updated_at":"2026-09-18T15:00:00.495739+00:00"}
